---
// Info page: SPIVA active-vs-index scorecard tables, from the reduced
// dataset in src/data/generated/spiva.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import InfoNote from "../../components/InfoNote.astro";
import { spiva } from "../../data/generated/spiva";
import "../../styles/info.css";

const H = spiva.horizons; // [1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20]
const tenIdx = H.indexOf(10);

const at = (catId: string, h: number) => {
  for (const g of spiva.groups) {
    const c = g.categories.find((x) => x.id === catId);
    if (c) return c.rates[H.indexOf(h)];
  }
  return null;
};

const largeCap10 = at("large-cap", 10); // 85.59
const allDom20 = at("all-domestic", 20); // 95.01
const largeCap1 = at("large-cap", 1); // 78.78

const fmt = (v: number | null) => (v == null ? "—" : `${Math.round(v)}%`);
const sourceNote = `${spiva.source} Figures are the share of actively managed funds that underperformed their benchmark (absolute return). As of ${spiva.asOf}.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Active vs. Index: the SPIVA Scorecard"
  description="What share of professional, actively managed funds fail to beat a simple index — by fund category and time horizon, from the SPIVA U.S. Scorecard. The evidence behind low-cost indexing."
>
  <section class="container ref-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Info · Reference</p>
    <h1>How often does active management beat the index?</h1>
    <p class="ref-lede">
      Twice a year, S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices publishes the <strong>SPIVA
      Scorecard</strong> — a tally of how many professional, actively managed
      funds actually beat the plain index they're measured against. The numbers
      below are the share that <em>fell short</em>. The pattern is remarkably
      consistent: the longer the clock runs, the more managers fall behind.
    </p>
    <p class="ref-asof">
      Data as of <strong>{spiva.asOf}</strong>
      <InfoNote text={sourceNote} />
      <span class="ref-asof-sep">·</span>
      <a href="#sources">source &amp; method</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container ref-block">
    <ul class="ref-stats">
      <li class="ref-stat">
        <span class="ref-stat-value">{fmt(largeCap10)}</span>
        <span class="ref-stat-label"
          >of US large-cap funds trailed the S&amp;P 500 over the last
          <strong>10 years</strong></span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="ref-stat">
        <span class="ref-stat-value">{fmt(allDom20)}</span>
        <span class="ref-stat-label"
          >of all US stock funds trailed their benchmark over
          <strong>20 years</strong></span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="ref-stat">
        <span class="ref-stat-value">{fmt(largeCap1)}</span>
        <span class="ref-stat-label"
          >trailed over just <strong>1 year</strong> — even short-term, most
          don't win</span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container ref-block">
    <h2>The full scorecard</h2>
    <p class="ref-prose">
      Each number is the percentage of funds in that category that
      <strong>underperformed</strong> their benchmark index over the period, so
      higher means worse for active management. A blank means SPIVA doesn't report
      that category at that horizon. The 10-year column is highlighted as a
      sensible middle horizon.
    </p>

    <div class="ref-tablewrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Fund category</th>
            <th scope="col">Benchmark</th>
            {
              H.map((h, i) => (
                <th scope="col" class:list={["num", { hi: i === tenIdx }]}>
                  {h}y
                </th>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            spiva.groups.map((g) => (
              <>
                <tr class="ref-group-row">
                  <th scope="rowgroup" colspan={2 + H.length}>
                    {g.label}
                  </th>
                </tr>
                {g.categories.map((c) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{c.name}</td>
                    <td class="ref-sub ref-mono">{c.benchmark}</td>
                    {c.rates.map((r, i) => (
                      <td class:list={["num", { hi: i === tenIdx }]}>{fmt(r)}</td>
                    ))}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="ref-caption">
      Above 50%, more than half the professionals in a category lost to a fund
      that simply bought the index and held it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container ref-block">
    <h2>Why this happens</h2>
    <div class="ref-prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Costs are a near-certainty; outperformance isn't.</strong> A
          fund's fees and trading costs come out every year, whether or not its
          picks beat the market. Over a decade that steady drag is hard to
          overcome — see <a href="/info/fund-fees">fund fees over time</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Active management is a zero-sum game before costs.</strong> For
          every investor who beats the market, another must lag it, because
          together they <em>are</em> the market. After costs, the average active
          dollar must trail a low-cost index dollar — an arithmetic point, not a
          forecast (W. Sharpe, 1991).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Winners are hard to identify in advance.</strong> A fund near
          the top over one stretch is roughly as likely as any other to lag over
          the next. Past performance genuinely doesn't predict future
          performance.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this says beating the index is impossible — a minority of funds
        do, and this is a description of the odds, not advice about what you
        should hold.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div id="sources">
    <Sources
      datasets={["spiva"]}
      studies={["sharpe1991", "famaFrench2010", "malkiel1973"]}
      title="Sources &amp; method"
      intro="Failure rates are transcribed directly from the SPIVA U.S. Scorecard (absolute-return tables). Refresh when S&P Dow Jones Indices publishes a new scorecard (npm run data:spiva)."
    />
  </div>

  <section class="container ref-related">
    <h2 class="ref-section-heading">Keep exploring</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid--2 ref-related-grid">
      <li>
        <a href="/tools/beat-the-market" class="card">
          <h3>Play with the horizon</h3>
          <p>
            The Can-You-Outsmart-the-Market tool turns this scorecard into a
            slider — pick a category and horizon and watch the failure rate climb.
          </p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/tools/fees" class="card">
          <h3>See what fees cost</h3>
          <p>
            The Fees &amp; Inflation tool shows how the yearly drag that sinks
            most active funds compounds against a balance over decades.
          </p>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
